Consume engine method constness in the const-receiver rules: the data now arrives via MethodInfo::is_const, but closing B116's engine-receiver exclusion is a new false-positive surface that needs its own variant sweep


## Filed by lane-enum on completing B124/B125, 2026-09-01

## What changed to make this possible

`MethodInfo::is_const` is now populated for the 301 of 1002 Core class methods
whose `decl` ends in ` const`. B124's stale comment is corrected. So the data
arrives — and **that is exactly why this ticket needs a guard rail rather than a
one-liner.**

## The prize and the price

**Prize:** B116 excludes the entire engine-receiver population by provenance.
That exclusion was argued partly on "no const-qualifier metadata exists", which
is no longer true. Closing it would extend the const-receiver rule to the
largest population it currently cannot see.

**Price, all three numbers already measured:**

- **807** engine receivers excluded by provenance (B116) — the population a gate
  reading "not known const" as "non-const" fires on.
- **80** handle-returning engine sites reached (B121).
- **3** of those provably indistinguishable from genuine views: `string::Split`
  returns a fresh `string[]@`, `Json::Value::Get` returns a real view, and both
  are `returntypeflags` 0 with a `@`.

## DO NOT let this be a one-liner because the data exists now

Run the variant sweep, the same shape B116, B120 and B121 all used: keep the
current answer, record every disagreement across the fleet, **read** the
disagreements, then decide. Report **exposure** as well as emits — the live
workspace surface has measured a structural zero four times running and a fifth
zero means nothing without reach. See the hazards entry.

## A hypothesis worth testing first, from the histogram

**The residual is ONLY the handle case.** By-value engine returns are fully
decided by `returntypeflags` (0 → 865 by value or handle, 3 → 123 `T&`,
7 → 14 `const T&`), so a rule restricted to **non-handle returns** may be
shippable with a far smaller surface than the full 807.

That is a hypothesis from a histogram, **not a measurement.** It needs the sweep
like everything else. Its value is that it suggests a cheaper first increment
than "all or nothing", not that it can skip the work.

## Related

B121's `residual_fresh_handles_are_documented_not_fixed` is the test that pins
the indistinguishable case. If this ticket finds a way to separate fresh handles
from views, that test is designed to fail — which is the signal, not a break.


## AMENDED by B137's game probe, 2026-09-01 — one claim in this ticket is superseded

B130's report said consuming the Nadeo loader's hardcoded `false` would have
fired "on every correctly-written const Nadeo call". **That population looks
empty.** B137 probed the engine with all three Core controls passing:

    const CMwNod@             n.MwAddRef()       REJECT
    CMwNod@ (non-const)       n.MwAddRef()       ACCEPT   <- isolates constness
    const CGameCtnBlock@      b.IsGhostBlock()   REJECT   (pure getter)
    const CGameCtnMediaBlock@ b.GetKeysCount()   REJECT   (pure getter)
    const CGameCtnChallenge@  c.MapName  (read)  REJECT

So the old hardcoded `false` was **factually right on every shape probed** —
consuming it would have produced TRUE positives, not false ones.

**B130's conclusion is unchanged and the exclusion still stands**, for the reason
that survives: *a value that is right by coincidence is not knowledge.* Five
probed members do not license a claim about 3084, and the dump does not say.
`None` is the honest state, and B137 made it representable.

The probe also explains the fleet's zero Nadeo exposure as a **fact rather than a
corpus accident**: a `const CMwNod@` receiver cannot be read through at all, so
almost no correct code has one. That is why nobody writes it — and it means the
payoff from ever resolving Nadeo constness is small.